### What does Collectors.joining do?

First you project each employee to its email with `map(Employee::getEmail)`, producing a stream of email strings. Then the terminal collector `Collectors.joining(", ")` concatenates them into a single string, inserting the delimiter between consecutive elements but not at the very start or very end.

### How do you extract emails as a comma-separated string?

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class JoiningEmails {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> staff = List.of(
            new Employee("Rajesh",  "rajesh@nbi.com",  "Loans",   new BigDecimal("95000")),
            new Employee("Priya",   "priya@nbi.com",   "IT",      new BigDecimal("120000")),
            new Employee("Amit",    "amit@nbi.com",    "Finance", new BigDecimal("85000")));

        String emailList = staff.stream()
            .map(Employee::getEmail)
            .collect(Collectors.joining(", "));

        System.out.println(emailList);
    }
}
```

```text
rajesh@nbi.com, priya@nbi.com, amit@nbi.com
```

### What are the three overloads of joining?

| Overload | Example | Result |
|---|---|---|
| joining() | joining() | "rajesh@nbi.compriya@nbi.comamit@nbi.com" |
| joining(delimiter) | joining(", ") | "rajesh@nbi.com, priya@nbi.com, amit@nbi.com" |
| joining(delim, prefix, suffix) | joining(", ", "[", "]") | "[rajesh@nbi.com, priya@nbi.com, amit@nbi.com]" |

```java
// Bracketed list for a summary report
String bracketed = staff.stream()
    .map(Employee::getEmail)
    .collect(Collectors.joining(", ", "[", "]"));

System.out.println(bracketed);
// [rajesh@nbi.com, priya@nbi.com, amit@nbi.com]
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: a null email in the stream throws NullPointerException during concatenation.

```java
// WRONG: if any email is null, joining crashes
String emails = staff.stream()
    .map(Employee::getEmail)  // might return null
    .collect(Collectors.joining(", ")); // NPE!

// CORRECT: filter out nulls first
String emails = staff.stream()
    .map(Employee::getEmail)
    .filter(java.util.Objects::nonNull)
    .collect(Collectors.joining(", "));
```

### How is joining different from reduce for string concatenation?

| Approach | Performance | Simplicity |
|---|---|---|
| Collectors.joining(", ") | O(n) via StringJoiner | One line, clean |
| reduce("", (a, b) -> a + ", " + b) | O(n^2) via String copies | Bug-prone (leading comma) |

```java
// Joining: O(n), clean, no leading-comma bug
staff.stream().map(Employee::getEmail)
    .collect(Collectors.joining(", "));

// Reduce: O(n^2), must guard leading comma manually
staff.stream().map(Employee::getEmail)
    .reduce("", (a, b) -> a.isEmpty() ? b : a + ", " + b);
```

### What about joining non-string types?

joinig only works on `Stream<CharSequence>`. For non-string types, map to a string representation first.

```java
// Joining salary amounts as a string
String salaries = staff.stream()
    .map(e -> e.getSalary().toPlainString())
    .collect(Collectors.joining(" | "));

System.out.println(salaries);
// 95000 | 120000 | 85000
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Under the hood, `joining` uses a `StringJoiner`, which is efficient because it appends to a single growing buffer rather than creating a new string for each concatenation.
- For very large datasets, the resulting string could be huge; consider streaming output instead of building one giant string.
- joining is the idiomatic and efficient way to build delimited strings; never use `reduce` with string concatenation in production.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What happens if the stream is empty?" joining returns an empty string (or just prefix+suffix for the three-arg form).
- "Why not use reduce for string concatenation?" It is O(n^2) because each + creates a new String.
- "Can you add a prefix and suffix?" Yes, use the three-argument joining(delimiter, prefix, suffix).
